---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Ref from '../components/Ref.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Stranger Than Heaven Gameplay: Combat, Showbiz and Systems"
  description="How Stranger Than Heaven plays: two-handed combat on the triggers and bumpers, the show business loop, and systems named only by the achievement list."
  path="/gameplay/"
>
  <h1>Gameplay systems</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Two halves: fighting your way up through the underworld, and building a career as a showman. This page separates
    what SEGA has shown from what only the achievement list has named.
  </p>

  <h2>Fighting with two hands</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Makoto's left and right sides are controlled separately, so one arm can block while the other strikes
      <Ref ids={['sega-0507', 'xbox-0506']} />.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Input on an Xbox pad</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">RB and RT</th><td>Makoto's right arm and leg</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">LB and LT</th><td>Makoto's left arm and leg</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">LT and RT together</th><td>A tackle</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Hold, then release</th><td>A charged hit, stronger if released at the right moment</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Guard with one side</th><td>Block with one arm, then hit back with the other</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">As shown in the May 2026 broadcast. <Ref ids="xbox-0506" /></p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The arsenal mixes everyday tools (knives, hammers, mallets) with antique blades such as katanas and gadgets
      newly invented in each era. Upgrades are possible, and every weapon brings its own special attacks and passive
      abilities <Ref ids="xbox-0506" />.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Show business</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Makoto's other life is as a showman. The loop SEGA has described <Ref ids={['sega-0507', 'xbox-0506']} />:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Listen.</strong> Record everyday sounds around town: a broom, a passing train, a brawl. What you can hear changes with the era, the place and the time of day.</li>
      <li><strong>Compose.</strong> Work with composers to turn those sounds into original songs, choosing instruments and mood.</li>
      <li><strong>Scout.</strong> Follow rumours and overheard talk to find singers and musicians.</li>
      <li><strong>Stage.</strong> Pick a five-piece band from your roster, set the running order and adjust the production, lighting included.</li>
      <li><strong>Tour.</strong> Take the show around the country to win audiences.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The Deluxe Upgrade's "Skilled Performers" and "Stage Production Items" plug into this side of the game. See
      <a href="/editions/">the editions</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Named only in the achievement list</h2>
  <p class="notice">
    Everything in this section comes from the <a href="/achievements/">achievement list that surfaced in September 2026</a>.
    SEGA has not described these systems, so the names are real but how they work is not known.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">What the list says</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Arts</th><td>Abilities you learn. The top achievement asks for 70.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Health and Willpower</th><td>Two stats with levels. The top achievement asks for level 50 in both.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Weapon proficiency</th><td>Levels per weapon, and a separate one for bare hands. Level 10 is asked for in each.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Forging and awakening</th><td>Weapons are forged, then awakened, up to a limit. One achievement asks for a weapon taken all the way.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Weapon kinds</th><td>At least 100 different kinds to own.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Livelihood missions</th><td>A set of missions separate from substories. At least 30.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Ideas</th><td>Something you collect, at least 50 of them, listed with the show business achievements.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Act-specific jobs</th><td>Warehouse security in Act 1, street fighting in Act 2, black markets in Act 3, underground fights in Act 4.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Camera</th><td>You take photos and develop them.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Menko and beigoma</th><td>Collectibles. Menko are cards slapped down to flip an opponent's; beigoma are small spinning tops. At least 70 of the two combined.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Minigames</th><td>At least 10 different ones.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Substories and minigames</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Each era has its own pastimes. The ones named so far are arm-wrestling, dice and card games in Kokura and target
      shooting in Osaka <Ref ids="xbox-0506" />. In a September 2026 interview the developers added that some
      minigames carry over from one era to the next, while others exist only in the Kokura period
      <Ref ids="automaton" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      Substories exist in every act: the achievement list has one entry per city for finishing them all. The
      <a href="/acts/">act pages</a> collect what is known city by city. What the game does not have, according to
      the studio, is a cabaret club or karaoke. That is part of
      <a href="/like-a-dragon-connection/">why it is not sold as a Like a Dragon game</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
